WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.720
- My brain doesn't work most of the time,

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.223
and I can't describe it
any better than that.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.220
The things I used to love doing,

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.060
I can't do anymore, because
I don't remember how.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:16.060
I so badly want to be who I was pre-COVID,

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:17.760
and I try to be that person,

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.170
and it's just not there.

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:21.960
- [Narrator] Rebecca
Meyer is one of thousands

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.230
of coronavirus survivors who are plagued

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.140
with long-term cognitive health issues

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.070
associated with COVID-19.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.780
- What's really concerning
is the vast spectrum

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.690
of symptoms that fluctuate over time,

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.660
even neurological and psychologic effects.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.070
- [Narrator] The severity of these issues

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.390
and how long they last is still unclear.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.150
But, that's starting to change.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.340
New research shows that the coronavirus

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:46.660
may have more wide-ranging effects

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:48.510
on the brain than previously thought.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.040
- We know that it affects the brain

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.690
because of some of the symptoms

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:55.560
that the patients are reporting.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.510
They're reporting memory issues,

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.740
or they're reporting forgetfulness,

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:02.740
they're reporting some brain fog,

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:06.120
they're reporting some
anxiety, some depression.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.920
- [Narrator] Doctors say some patients

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.160
are also experiencing inattention

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.810
and symptoms that resemble
chronic fatigue syndrome,

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.970
and that many are reporting them months

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.560
after initially getting sick.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.960
- Patients that, often times, are

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.230
in their 20s, 30s, 40s,

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:22.760
never had these symptoms before,

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.900
or if they did, they were mild.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.160
- [Narrator] This isn't the first time

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:27.680
researchers have seen this.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.070
Other viral outbreaks
like SARS, MERS, Ebola,

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.070
H1N1, and the Spanish flu

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:35.110
have also caused long-term symptoms

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:36.650
related to the nervous system.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.490
- We know that from SARS,

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:41.840
there were long-term cognitive changes.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.120
There are 50 or 100 patients

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.260
that were able to be followed up,

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.890
and we saw long-term
neuropsychiatric changes,

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.680
changes in levels of
post-exertional fatigue,

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.950
levels of depression and anxiety,

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.980
levels of ability to go back to work.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.820
- [Narrator] Long-term COVID-19 research

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.040
is still in its early stages.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.530
Right now, scientists still aren't sure

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.850
how the coronavirus affects
neurological functioning.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.320
In certain infections,

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.720
viruses can actually enter the brain.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.840
If the brain's immune system reacts,

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:16.360
that can lead to swelling,

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.500
also known as encephalitis.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.840
While this can happen
with the coronavirus,

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.890
researchers say it's very rare.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:24.630
Instead, it's more likely

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.170
that the virus does its damage

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.290
through an overactive immune response

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:29.400
throughout the body.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.360
This can also lead to inflammation,

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:32.750
which can harm neurons,

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:35.410
cells that are essential
to cognitive functioning.

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.680
Here's a closer look at how that happens.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.870
The virus that causes COVID-19

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.410
usually enters the body

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.730
through the nose, mouth, or eyes.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:46.480
From there, it can infect
the respiratory system,

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.820
traveling down the trachea to the lungs,

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:50.430
where it can enter the bloodstream.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.010
To fight off this infection,

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:54.770
the body sends a flood
of immune system cells

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:57.020
and molecules known as cytokines.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.600
These cause inflammation,

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:01.750
which can make it easier
for blood to form clots.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:04.130
When these clots build up,
they can prevent oxygen

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:06.530
from reaching vital organs,
including the brain,

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:08.100
which can cause cells to die.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.560
In the most severe cases,

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.250
this can lead to strokes.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.100
But it's not just the lack of oxygen

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.650
that can cause damage.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.380
Inflammation can also harm

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.820
the protective barrier
that shields the brain,

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.910
making the organ more vulnerable.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.090
- The brain is protected

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.620
by something we call
the blood-brain barrier,

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.900
and the blood-brain barrier is

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.600
not really a physical barrier,

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.460
but it's a biochemical barrier,

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.300
and it allows certain things to go in.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.180
Cells can go in and out.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.130
Certain nutrients can go in and out.

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.110
- [Narrator] The blood-brain
barrier does a good job

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:42.890
of preventing toxins and pathogens

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:44.170
from entering the brain.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:46.760
But new research shows
that the coronavirus

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:48.780
can infect and disrupt the cells

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.470
that line the barrier walls.

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:52.700
This is called the endothelium.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.300
This disruption causes
the body's immune system

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.130
to kick further into overdrive,

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:59.190
leading to inflammation in the brain.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:03.100
- Imagine a road that is paved

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:06.090
by endothelial cells, and along that road,

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.530
you have immune cells, like cars,

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:11.253
that are rolling along that road.

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:15.870
If a virus or something comes along

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:18.170
and destroys that road,

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:20.200
the lining, the endothelial cells,

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:25.200
and it becomes rough and
difficult to ride along,

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:28.300
and the immune cells
now start bumping along,

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.910
they become activated, and they now

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:34.130
start producing all sorts of free radicals

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:36.240
and inflammatory factors.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.750
That repair process,
if it goes on too long,

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.100
you now have ongoing chronic inflammation.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:44.850
We suspect that that is what happens

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:48.040
in the ongoing process of long haulers.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:49.840
- [Narrator] And this isn't the only way

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:52.310
that inflammation can
attack the nervous system.

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:55.490
Cells supporting the
olfactory neurons in the nose

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:57.730
can also be damaged by the infection.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:00.560
Scientists suspect that
when these cells die,

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:03.820
patients might experience
a loss of taste and smell.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:05.400
Research from other infections

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:06.950
shows that inflammatory signals

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:09.020
can make their way up
nerves into the brain,

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:10.820
causing even more inflammation.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:11.990
These are some of the ways

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:13.470
that scientists think inflammation

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:14.920
can affect the nervous system,

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:15.980
and they could explain

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:17.630
why so many long-haul patients

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:20.350
are experiencing neurological problems.

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:21.183
So what does this mean

00:05:21.183 --> 00:05:23.160
for those with persisting symptoms?

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.560
Past epidemics offer some clues.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.360
Researchers studying the 1918 Spanish flu

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.630
found that a number of
survivors showed symptoms

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:33.070
related to Parkinson's decades later.

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:35.720
More recently, physicians
have reported tremors

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:37.920
in a few COVID-19 patients,

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.200
but much of the research
is still in animals.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.250
- [Dr. Tansey] It may
range from some effects

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:43.600
that are temporary

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.270
to some that may be permanent,

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:48.840
including loss of certain circuits

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.390
that may not come back.

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:52.580
Those may be associated with things

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.980
like dementia or motor symptoms,

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.840
things like Parkinsonism,

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:00.000
things like Alzheimer's
and other dementias.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.460
- [Narrator] But this idea is preliminary.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:05.330
- It's a prediction based on past events,

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:09.170
where we know that viruses
that affect the brain,

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:12.700
directly or indirectly, may have effects.

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:14.080
And so, we'll have to see.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.570
It'll be a matter of time

00:06:16.547 --> 00:06:18.670
in trying to test it.

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:20.930
- [Narrator] Even so,
scientists say these findings

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:22.810
are an important step forward,

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:25.120
and that the research
could help find answers,

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.070
not just for COVID-19 patients,

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:29.410
but for those with other
brain diseases too.

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:30.640
- One reason to hope

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.930
is that I also have seen patients

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:36.800
who have recovered within weeks to months.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:38.650
People who

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.170
may have responded well to some treatment

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:45.430
for some underlying
associated brain fog symptoms,

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:47.530
it seems that the underlying cognitive

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:50.040
dysfunction has been improving

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:55.040
without any strong immunomodulatory
drugs, for example.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:57.573
That said,

00:06:58.750 --> 00:06:59.780
the jury is still out.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:02.560
- Hopefully these issues that I'm having,

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:04.620
and other people, it's not lifelong.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:06.580
That's what we're hoping.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:08.030
It feels like it is right now.

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:10.840
It feels like we're gonna be sick forever.

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:14.175
We just have to hope
that that's not the case.

00:07:14.175 --> 00:07:16.925
(dramatic music)

